{
  "id": "lowfield-cnp-eeg-alpha-test",
  "name": "Cnp occipital alpha EEG test (Cook design)",
  "category": "research",
  "evidence": "unverified",
  "fidelity": "timing",
  "dose": {"value": 200, "unit": "uT", "plane": "centre", "cite": "Cook 2004 p.2 [197]"},
  "origin": "Cook, Thomas and Prato (Lawson Health Research Institute, London, Ontario)",
  "uT": 5,
  "summary": "A research probe after Cook, Thomas and Prato's EEG study (Cook 2004): 5 min without field, the 15-min Cnp exposure (here on all eight ring coils), then 10 min without field, for recording resting alpha before, during and after exposure. Cook's field was +/-200 uT peak, uniform over the seated person; this helmet gives 5 uT at 20 mm from each coil, so only the timing is copied. Compare with a blinded sham session.",
  "notes": "Cook 2004 exposed 20 seated people for 15 min to a Cnp pulsed field of +/-200 uT peak, uniform over the person, from the vertical pair of three orthogonal square Helmholtz-like coils of 2, 1.75 and 1.5 m (p.2 [197]). The session was 2.5 min eyes open, 2.5 min eyes closed, 15 min field, 5 min sampling, 2.5 min eyes closed and 2.5 min eyes open (Table 1 p.2 [197]). The field was the pulse of US 6,234,953, 'combined in an 853 ms segment which includes 18 pulses', with pauses of 110, 220, 330 and 1200 ms (p.3 [198]). Field and sham sessions were counterbalanced; alpha (8 to 13 Hz) was recorded at O1, Oz, O2 and P3, Pz, P4. This program plays that outline and sequence: 5 min off, 15 min of the 5272-ms cycle, 10 min off. The patent table's 18 whole pulses span 849 ms; each segment is filled with zero to Cook's 853 ms (the sources do not explain the 4 ms). The eight ring coils in phase do not reproduce Cook's uniform vertical field. The EEG recording, the analysis and the sham allocation are left to you. This program's setting is the peak field 20 mm in front of each coil. At 5 uT it is 40 times weaker there than Cook's field. The results come from one lab. Cook 2004 reported occipital alpha higher after exposure (parietal only marginal) and a marginal drop about 7 min later. Cook 2005 (alpha lower during exposure) and the second arm of Cook 2009 used a sequence with a 5000-ms fourth pause (Cook 2005 p.3 [369]; Cook 2009 p.2 [10]), so those results do not belong to this program. The app's Blind option picks real or sham at random for each session, so two sessions may not be one of each; for Cook's counterbalanced design have a helper decide which session is the sham and keep the person unaware. Fix the analysis before unblinding. Coil currents can couple into EEG leads, so test with a phantom first (Wang 2019 p.5). Not for people with epilepsy; stop on distress.",
  "setting": "Seated and resting, with EEG at O1, Oz, O2 and P3, Pz, P4. In Cook 2004 the first and the last 2.5 min were eyes open, the rest eyes closed (Table 1 p.2 [197]).",
  "schedule": "Two sessions, field and sham, in counterbalanced order with the person blinded; analyse alpha (8-13 Hz) before, during and after exposure.",
  "targets": [
    "primary-visual-cortex",
    "precuneus"
  ],
